      <section id="co2-beverage-aerosol-mechanics" class="section">
        <h2>CO2 Beverage Aerosol Mechanics</h2>
        <p>Carbon dioxide behaves quite differently than nitrous oxide when dissolved in food systems, owing to its high solubility in water and its chemical reactivity with aqueous matrices. In beverage aerosols, carbon dioxide functions both as an expelling agent and as a carbonating medium, introducing fizz and acidity to the product. The physics of this system is governed by Henry's Law, which determines the concentration of gas dissolved in the liquid based on the partial pressure of the gas headspace.</p>
        <p>When carbon dioxide dissolves in water, a portion of the gas undergoes hydration to form carbonic acid (H₂CO₃), which subsequently dissociates into bicarbonate and hydrogen ions, lowering the pH of the liquid. This chemical transition gives carbonated beverages their characteristic tartness. In an aerosol container, the high pressure forces a large volume of carbon dioxide into solution; once dispensed, the pressure relief drives the gas out of solution, forming bubbles of effervescence. Because carbon dioxide does not dissolve in lipids to the same extent as nitrous oxide, it is unsuitable for creating stable whipped creams, as the bubble expansion in water occurs too rapidly to be stabilized by fat matrices, leading to immediate foam collapse.</p>
        <p>The selection of the propellant—or a mixture of propellants—directly impacts the final sensory attributes and physical performance of the dispensed product. The physical differences between nitrous oxide and carbon dioxide must be carefully analyzed when engineering food aerosol systems, as shown in the comparison table below.</p>

