          <p style="text-align: center; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 8px; font-style: italic;">
            Figure 2: Close-up view of a precision food-grade aerosol valve assembly and actuator nozzle under laboratory inspection, emphasizing the clean, sanitary geometry required for food contact.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>Aseptic filling lines represent the pinnacle of food aerosol technology, utilizing hydrogen peroxide sterilization for the containers and valves, and operating entirely within a sterile enclosure. This setup allows whipped cream to be stored at room temperature prior to opening, extending shelf life without chemical preservatives. To explore general aerosol packaging concepts, manufacturing guidelines, and standard structures, the European Aerosol Federation (<a href="https://aerosol.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FEA</a>) offers technical resources that outline manufacturing line design and compliance protocols.</p>
        <p>For a detailed overview of general aerosol engineering concepts, please consult our foundational guide on <a href="/aerosols/" target="_top">aerosol propellants and valves</a>. Further reading on propellant formulations can be found in our technical articles on <a href="/aerosol-propellants-explained-lpg-vs-dme-vs-hfc-vs-n2-vs-co2/" target="_top">aerosol propellants explained</a>, our comparative review of <a href="/types-of-aerosol-systems-explained/" target="_top">types of aerosol systems</a>, and our safety guidelines on <a href="/aerosol-can-safety-explained/" target="_top">aerosol can safety</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <section id="faq" class="box" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 30px; margin: 40px 0;">
        <h2 style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 24px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; color: var(--text-primary);">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px;">
          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Why is nitrous oxide preferred over carbon dioxide for whipping cream?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Nitrous oxide is highly lipophilic, meaning it dissolves readily in the fat phase of cream under pressure without altering the taste. Upon dispensing, the sudden drop in pressure causes the dissolved gas to expand rapidly, whipping the fat matrix into a light, stable foam. Carbon dioxide, in contrast, is highly hydrophilic, dissolves in the water phase to form carbonic acid, and creates a sour taste and effervescence rather than a stable foam structure.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What safety hazards are associated with food-grade N2O aerosol cans?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Food-grade nitrous oxide aerosol cans operate under high pressure and present risk of bursting if exposed to heat exceeding standard limits, as compressed gases do not maintain a constant vapor pressure like liquefied gases. Safety hazards are mitigated by compliance with transport regulations, pressure testing of empty cans, and implementing temperature protection thresholds. Proper ventilation and handling equipment are mandatory on filling lines to prevent accidental gas inhalation.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">How does Henry's Law govern the behavior of carbon dioxide in beverage aerosols?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Henry's Law states that at a constant temperature, the amount of a given gas dissolved in a given type and volume of liquid is directly proportional to the partial pressure of that gas in equilibrium with that liquid. In beverage aerosols, carbon dioxide is dissolved under high pressure in the aqueous matrix; once the actuator is pressed, the local partial pressure drops, causing the dissolved carbon dioxide to desorb from the liquid phase and form effervescent bubbles.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What sanitary requirements apply to food-grade aerosol valves?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Food-grade aerosol valves must utilize materials certified for food contact, such as FDA-compliant elastomers (silicone, Buna-N, or EPDM gaskets) and stainless steel springs. The valve architecture must prevent product entrapment to avoid microbial contamination. Sanitary designs dictate that the actuator nozzle is easily cleanable, self-draining, and free of dead zones where residue can spoil.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">How is overrun controlled in commercial aerosol whipped cream?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Overrun, which represents the volume expansion percentage of whipped cream, is controlled by balancing the pressure of nitrous oxide, the lipid structure of the cream emulsion, and the design of the expansion chamber in the valve. A high-lipid content provides the necessary matrix to trap expanding gas bubbles. The nozzle geometry and actuator expansion chamber regulate the shearing force, ensuring consistent foam texture and stability without structural collapse.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="padding-bottom: 8px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What are the regulatory purity standards for food contact aerosol propellants?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Food contact aerosol propellants must comply with strict purity standards defined by global bodies, such as FDA Title 21 CFR and EFSA specifications (E942 for nitrous oxide, E290 for carbon dioxide). The gas must have a minimum purity of 99.0% or 99.9%, with strict limitations on contaminants such as carbon monoxide, nitrogen dioxide, moisture, and volatile organic compounds to ensure safety and prevent off-flavors.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

